Adaptive and Explainable Fraud Detection

Research Project by Dhanush Reddy Mittapalli
Independent Researcher

Overview

This project investigates temporal distribution drift and missingness-associated fraud patterns in IEEE-CIS e-commerce transactions.

Research Objectives

1. Develop a machine-learning fraud detection model.
2. Evaluate performance using chronological data splitting.
3. Investigate temporal distribution drift.
4. Analyze missingness-associated fraud patterns.
5. Compare global and adaptive threshold policies.
6. Improve reproducibility and explainability.

Dataset

The research uses the IEEE-CIS Fraud Detection dataset.

Dataset access: https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/ieee-fraud-detection

Note: The original dataset is not included in this repository. Users must obtain it through Kaggle and follow its terms of use.

Methodology

- Chronological train, validation and test evaluation
- XGBoost classification
- Threshold optimization
- Population Stability Index (PSI)
- Jensen-Shannon divergence
- Kolmogorov-Smirnov diagnostics
- Missingness and interaction analysis

Current Status

Research code and documentation are being organized for reproducibility. Further validation and improvements are planned.

